{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbqXy76q2Sc7STZRKe3h", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Lists** are mutable. **Tuples** are immutable.\n\n| | List | Tuple |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` |\n| Mutable | Yes: you can append, remove, or assign to items | No: contents can't change after creation |\n| Hashable | No, so it can't be a dict key or set member | Yes, if all its elements are hashable |\n| Performance | Slightly more memory, slightly slower | Smaller and a bit faster |\n| Typical use | Homogeneous collections that change | Fixed records, such as `(x, y)` or `(name, age)`, and return values |\n\n```python\nlst = [1, 2, 3]\nlst[0] = 99          # ok\nlst.append(4)        # ok\n\ntup = (1, 2, 3)\ntup[0] = 99          # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```\n\n**Details:**\n- A single-element tuple needs a trailing comma: `(1,)`. Without it, `(1)` is just the integer 1.\n- Tuples are only shallowly immutable. If a tuple holds a list, that list can still change: `t = ([1], 2); t[0].append(5)` works.\n- Both are ordered, indexable, sliceable, and iterable.\n- Tuples support unpacking: `x, y = (1, 2)`.\n\n**Rule of thumb:** use a tuple for a fixed group of values, and a list for a collection that will grow, shrink, or be modified."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7218, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7218}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "c49e81be-b29e-4d1f-b827-5ad9d088324f", "uuid": "fc5f65b7-ffb0-4584-8168-e946c15da0a7", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T16:32:08.677Z",